521: validation would require additional command-result or formal-source coordinates;
522: none is among the fields read in this lemma.  Thus the table is complete for the
523: imported interface of this paper and correct at the stated evidence level: exact
524: as to path, commit, label, line range, method, date, and summary mode, and
525: bounded as to mathematical restatement.\end{proof}
526: 
527: \begin{proposition}[Manual source-interface non-escalation]
528: \label{prop:manual-source-interface-non-escalation}
529: Let $I$ be the imported discovery-gate interface certified by
530: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}.  Any later use of an
531: imported source label in this paper is a consequence of $I$ only if it is a
532: function of the finite fields recorded in Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}:
533: the source path, pinned commit, label, line range, extraction date, bounded
534: summary, and evidence-level qualifier.  A proposed stronger use---for example an
535: exact import of the original source theorem statement, a fresh BEDC or Lean
536: rebuild, an axiom-purity statement, or a semantic validation of the imported
537: source theorem---is accepted by the finite-record safety interface only after the
538: submitted record surface is enlarged by the corresponding source-statement,
539: formal-build, or command-run coordinate and by the assigned pass records for that
540: coordinate.  Without that enlargement the stronger use is rejected as a finite
541: bad subrecord and the accepted consequence remains the manual path-verified
542: bounded interface.
543: \end{proposition}
544: 
545: \begin{proof}
546: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} identifies exactly the
547: finite fields in $I$ and states that the summaries are bounded paraphrases rather
548: than replacements of the original source statements.  Therefore two possible
549: review states that agree on those fields but differ in an unsubmitted source
550: checkout, proof-assistant state, axiom audit, or executable extraction log give
551: the same values to every coordinate read by $I$.  By
552: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, no claim depending on
553: one of the differing unsubmitted coordinates is a consequence of the finite
554: interface record.
555: 
556: If a stronger use is made load-bearing, then it strictly enlarges the original
557: source-interface reading in the stronger-reading preorder of
558: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}: exact source-statement import
559: adds an evidence-surface coordinate, a BEDC or Lean rebuild adds formal-source or
560: command-run coordinates, axiom-purity adds an assigned gate and pass record, and
561: semantic validation adds a new claim-kind or boundary coordinate.  The normal
562: form of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} says that such a
563: genuine strengthening is accepted exactly when the added finite coordinates and
564: their gate records are supplied.  If they are absent,
565: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} returns the missing
566: coordinate as a finite bad subrecord.  Thus the source import used downstream,
567: including in Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, remains the manual
568: path-verified bounded interface and nothing stronger.\end{proof}
569: 
570: \begin{proposition}[Reviewable source-snapshot interface]
571: \label{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface}
572: The imported discovery-gate spine used in
573: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, has a reviewer-facing source
574: interface.  Namely, the tuple consisting of the pinned local source path, commit
575: \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b},
576: Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}, \path{source_interface_record.json},
577: and the supplemental copy
578: \path{review_bundle/source_snapshots/automated_theory_discovery_pipeline_calculus_3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b.tex}
579: is sufficient to inspect every imported source label used by this paper at the
580: path-verified evidence level.  The distribution mode for review is supplemental
581: upload of \path{review_bundle/} with the presentation-only submission.  No
582: public archive URL or DOI is part of the current theorem interface.
583: \end{proposition}
584: 
585: \begin{proof}
586: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} proves that the only
587: imported source labels used by the paper are the sixteen rows of
588: Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}, each with a fixed line range and bounded
589: summary.  The paragraph preceding that lemma fixes the original local source
590: path, source commit, extraction date, and JSON mirror.  The supplemental copy is
591: a file-level snapshot of the same source-interface document, named by the same
592: commit hash.  Therefore a reviewer does not need access to the author's absolute
593: path in order to inspect the imported interface: the table gives the labels and
594: line ranges, the JSON record gives the machine-readable binding, and the bundle
595: copy gives the text snapshot against which those ranges are checked.  The current reviewer-facing distribution mode is fixed by the proposition:
596: the bundle is uploadable supplemental material, not an unrecorded private
597: dependency, and no archival identifier is needed for the bounded interface
598: claim.\end{proof}
599: 
600: \begin{proposition}[Executable source-interface record comparison]
601: \label{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}
602: The source-interface table, its JSON mirror, and the bundled source snapshot give
603: one record-level imported discovery-gate interface for
604: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  The record fields are as
605: follows: the source commit is
606: \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b}; the bundled snapshot is
607: \path{review_bundle/source_snapshots/automated_theory_discovery_pipeline_calculus_3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b.tex};
608: the finite comparison script is
609: \path{review_bundle/verify_source_interface_record.py}; the recorded successful
610: comparison log is \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log};
611: the recorded SHA-256 digest checked by the script is
612: \path{a492900222dd66d154cdd5a79d2b2ed0b856ad1ec59e5dfce55dbc21875ed1b2};
613: the recorded snapshot length is 498 lines; for each of the sixteen line ranges in
614: \path{source_interface_record.json}, the corresponding imported label is recorded
615: inside the stated snapshot range; and the sixteen triples \emph{label, line range,
616: bounded summary} in Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record} are the sixteen JSON
617: records.  The verifier log uses two distinct commit fields: the imported external
618: newmath source commit is the pinned commit displayed above, while the local
619: publication-package commit is the Git \path{HEAD} of this paper workspace at the
620: time the comparison command is run.  Hence, at the finite record-comparison level, the manuscript table,
621: JSON record, review-bundle manifest, digest, line count, and bundled snapshot
622: define one consistent imported-source interface.  The comparison does not assert
623: semantic truth of the imported source theorems, does not perform a BEDC/Lean
624: source-theory extraction, does not reproduce the exact source theorem statements
625: as manuscript theorems, and does not replace the bounded paraphrase discipline of
626: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}.  Exact source statement
627: inspection, if desired by a reviewer, is supported only by the bundled snapshot
628: and line ranges; it is not an imported theorem obligation of this paper.
629: \end{proposition}
630: 
631: \begin{proof}
632: The proposition is a finite equality-and-containment check over submitted
633: records.  The script \path{review_bundle/verify_source_interface_record.py}
634: parses the sixteen rows of Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record} from this source,
635: loads \path{source_interface_record.json}, loads the
636: \path{pinned_source_snapshot} object of
637: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, and computes the SHA-256 digest
638: and line count of the bundled snapshot.  It then checks seven finite conditions:
639: the ordered manuscript rows are the ordered JSON records; the JSON source path
640: and commit equal the manifest's original path and commit; the ordered JSON line
641: ranges equal the manifest's \path{line_ranges_used}; the computed digest equals
642: the manifest digest; the computed line count equals the manifest line count; and for each declared range,
643: the corresponding \path{\label{...}} token occurs inside that inclusive snapshot
644: interval.
645: 
646: The recorded run in \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log}
647: has status \path{pass}.  Its counts are sixteen manuscript table rows, sixteen JSON
648: records, sixteen manifest ranges, snapshot line count 498, and digest
649: \path{a492900222dd66d154cdd5a79d2b2ed0b856ad1ec59e5dfce55dbc21875ed1b2}.  Its
650: \path{imported_newmath_source_commit} field is the external source commit
651: \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b}; its
652: \path{local_publication_package_commit} field is only provenance for the local
653: paper checkout used to run the verifier and is not a second imported-source pin.  Its
654: per-row label checks all report \path{label_in_range: true} for the sixteen labels
655: listed in Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}.  Therefore no accepted row can
656: be a table-only, JSON-only, manifest-only, or out-of-range source-interface row:
657: any such defect would violate one of the seven checked finite conditions and would
658: appear as a finite bad subrecord.  What remains unchecked is exactly outside the
659: claim: the script does not interpret or prove the imported theorem statements,
660: does not check BEDC semantic extraction, and does not check Lean or source-theory
661: completeness.  The manuscript does not quote the imported statements as exact
662: source theorem text.  The exact source text remains available only as the bundled
663: snapshot at the displayed line ranges.  Thus the executable comparison strengthens
664: the earlier manual audit into a reproducible equality-and-containment certificate
665: while preserving the bounded-paraphrase and non-semantic boundary.\end{proof}
666: 
667: \begin{theorem}[Bounded source-interface maximality]
668: \label{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}
669: Let $I_{\rm man}$ be the manual imported-source interface of
670: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}, and let $I_{\rm exe}$
671: be the executable finite comparison of
672: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}.  In the
673: finite-record safety interface of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface},
674: the strongest imported discovery-gate premise available from
675: $I_{\rm man}$ together with $I_{\rm exe}$ is exactly the following finite
676: certificate:
677: \begin{enumerate}
678: \item the imported source path and pinned source commit;
679: \item the sixteen ordered labels, line ranges, extraction date, and bounded
680: statement summaries in Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record};
681: \item equality of those ordered triples with \path{source_interface_record.json};
682: \item agreement with the review-bundle manifest on source path, source commit,
683: line ranges, snapshot digest, and snapshot line count; and
684: \item containment of each imported label token in its declared bundled-snapshot
685: line interval.
686: \end{enumerate}
687: Consequently every accepted use of an imported discovery-gate label in the main
688: theorem chain, in particular in Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface},
689: factors through this finite certificate.  Any reading that imports the exact
690: source theorem statements, asserts semantic truth of the imported theorems,
691: performs a BEDC or Lean rebuild, proves axiom purity, or certifies source-theory
692: completeness is a properly stronger reading and is rejected unless the submitted
693: surface is enlarged by the corresponding finite source-statement, command-run,
694: formal-build, axiom-audit, or semantic-check coordinate and by its assigned pass
695: records.
696: \end{theorem}
697: 
698: \begin{proof}
699: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} supplies exactly the
700: manual finite fields in (1) and (2), and it states that the row summaries are
701: bounded paraphrases rather than replacements for the full source statements.
702: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} supplies exactly
703: the executable finite fields in (3)--(5): the script compares the manuscript
704: table, JSON mirror, bundle manifest, digest, line count, and labelled intervals
705: and reports a finite bad subrecord on any failed comparison.  These two records
706: therefore have the displayed union as their complete positive content.
707: 
708: The verifier used in Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} can read only
709: submitted finite coordinates.  By Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-source-interface-non-escalation},
710: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-evidence-level-separation}, and
711: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, two review states
712: that agree on the displayed finite certificate but differ in an unsubmitted
713: source checkout, proof-assistant state, axiom audit, semantic extractor, or build
714: log are indistinguishable to the imported-source component of the certificate
715: constructor.  Hence every accepted imported-label premise in the theorem chain
716: factors through the displayed certificate.
717: 
718: Each excluded reading strictly adds a finite coordinate in the preorder of
719: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}: exact source-statement import
720: adds a source-text evidence coordinate; BEDC or Lean rebuilding adds a formal
721: source or command-run coordinate; axiom purity adds an audit coordinate and pass
722: row; and semantic source-theorem validation adds a semantic-check coordinate.
723: The finite extension-budget normal form,
724: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, accepts such a stronger
725: reading exactly when those added coordinates and pass records are present.
726: When they are absent, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}
727: classifies the missing coordinate as a finite bad subrecord.  Thus the displayed
728: certificate is maximal for the submitted source-interface surface while remaining
729: strictly bounded to record equality and label containment.
730: \end{proof}
731: 
732: \begin{corollary}[Downstream imported-label use factors through bounded rows]
733: \label{cor:downstream-imported-label-bounded-factorization}
734: Every load-bearing occurrence in this manuscript of an imported discovery-gate
735: label from Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record} factors through the bounded
736: source-interface certificate of
737: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}.  Hence a downstream proof
738: may use only the row's recorded path, pinned commit, label token, line range,
739: extraction date, bounded summary, evidence-level qualifier, equality with
740: \path{source_interface_record.json}, manifest agreement, and bundled-snapshot
741: label containment.  If such an occurrence is read instead as an exact quotation
742: of the source theorem, as a semantic import of its proof, or as a source-theory
743: rebuild claim, the reading is a properly stronger source-interface claim and is
744: not accepted without the corresponding added coordinates and pass records.
745: \end{corollary}
746: 
747: \begin{proof}
748: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality} identifies the maximal
749: finite certificate available from the manual row audit together with the
750: executable comparison.  The only imported labels available to later proofs are
751: the sixteen rows of Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}, by
752: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}; and
753: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-source-interface-non-escalation} says that any later
754: consequence of that interface must be a function of exactly those finite fields.
755: Thus every downstream occurrence factors through the displayed bounded
756: certificate.  A reading that imports an exact statement, a semantic proof, or a
757: source-theory rebuild adds a source-statement, semantic, formal-build, or
758: command-run coordinate absent from the certificate.  By
759: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} such a strengthening is
760: accepted exactly when the added coordinates and assigned pass records are
761: present; otherwise Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}
762: returns the missing coordinate as a finite bad subrecord.  This proves both the
763: factorization and the non-escalation clause.
764: \end{proof}
765: 
766: \begin{theorem}[Public-source reachability non-escalation]
767: \label{thm:public-source-reachability-non-escalation}
768: In the current source package, the citation \cite{bedc-newmath-source} and the
769: commit string \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b} serve only as the
770: public-source coordinate for the imported newmath workspace.  The load-bearing
771: source-interface premise used by Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}
772: remains the bundled finite record certified by
773: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}: path, commit string,
774: sixteen labels, line ranges, bounded summaries, JSON equality, manifest agreement,
775: snapshot digest and line count, and label containment in the bundled snapshot.
776: Unless the submitted surface additionally contains a dated public-repository
777: reachability and byte-equality log for the pinned commit and bundled snapshot, no
778: claim in this paper may infer that the public repository was reachable at review
779: time, that the public branch contains the pinned commit, or that public bytes are
780: equal to the bundled snapshot bytes.  If such a dated log is added, its only
781: upgrade is the finite reachability/equality coordinate named by that log; it still
782: does not prove semantic truth of the imported source theorems, Lean/BEDC rebuild
783: success, marker correctness, or axiom purity.
784: \end{theorem}
785: 
786: \begin{proof}
787: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality} identifies the complete
788: positive imported-source content currently supplied to the finite-record safety
789: theorem: it is a finite bundle-backed equality-and-containment certificate over
790: the manuscript table, \path{source_interface_record.json}, the review-bundle
791: manifest, and the bundled source snapshot.  None of those fields is a dated
792: network observation.  In particular, a commit string in a citation and a local
793: snapshot digest are not fields recording a public-repository command, remote URL,
794: environment, exit code, log path, fetched object identity, or byte comparison
795: against a public checkout.
796: 
797: Consider two review states that agree on every field listed in
798: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality} and on the bibliography
799: entry \cite{bedc-newmath-source}.  In the first state a reviewer also has a dated
800: successful remote-reachability and byte-equality log; in the second state that log
801: is absent.  The current verifier and theorem chain read the same finite
802: coordinates in both states, because the distinguishing public-reachability log is
803: not part of the submitted support surface.  By
804: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, a stronger conclusion
805: that distinguishes these states cannot be inferred from the common finite tuple.
806: Thus public reachability, public commit containment, and public byte equality are
807: not current consequences of the citation or bundled snapshot records.
808: 
809: If a later package adds the dated log, then the support tuple has a new finite
810: coordinate: the command or check performed, the public repository locator, the
811: source commit, the compared path or object, the environment, the exit code, the
812: log path, and the resulting digest or equality record.  The finite extension is
813: accepted exactly as an added coordinate under
814: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}; it does not add any of the
815: separate semantic, formal-build, marker, or axiom-audit coordinates excluded by
816: Theorem~\ref{thm:source-interface-semantic-completeness-obstruction}.  Hence the
817: only possible upgrade is the stated finite public reachability/equality reading.
818: \end{proof}
819: 
820: \begin{corollary}[Public-source coordinate exactness]
821: \label{cor:public-source-coordinate-exactness}
822: For the present source package, any load-bearing statement containing the public
823: source citation \cite{bedc-newmath-source} and the pinned commit
824: \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b} factors through exactly the
825: bounded coordinates of Theorem~\ref{thm:public-source-reachability-non-escalation}.
826: Thus the citation contributes only the public locator, pinned commit string, and
827: the already-recorded bundled source-interface rows.  It contributes neither a
828: dated public fetch, nor a branch-membership proof, nor public byte equality, nor
829: semantic validity of the imported statements.  A public-source reading is
830: upgraded if and only if the submitted support surface adds a finite
831: fetch-and-compare coordinate naming the remote locator, object or commit, compared
832: path, source state, environment, exit code, log path, and resulting byte or digest
833: comparison.
834: \end{corollary}
835: 
836: \begin{proof}
837: Theorem~\ref{thm:public-source-reachability-non-escalation} proves the negative
838: direction: the current finite tuple lacks the dated remote observation and byte
839: comparison fields, so public reachability, branch containment, and public byte
840: equality are not consequences of the citation or bundled snapshot.  The only
841: positive fields that remain are precisely the fields already read by
842: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}: public locator as a
843: bibliographic coordinate, pinned commit string, source path, labels, line ranges,
844: bounded summaries, manifest agreement, snapshot digest and line count, and label
845: containment in the bundled snapshot.
846: 
847: For the converse upgrade criterion, suppose the submitted surface is enlarged by
848: the stated fetch-and-compare coordinate and by its assigned pass row.  That is a
849: finite extension coordinate in the sense of
850: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, so the certificate may now
851: read exactly the remote-reachability and byte/digest-comparison fact recorded in
852: that row.  No other field is added: semantic source validity, Lean/BEDC rebuild
853: success, marker correctness, and axiom purity would require separate semantic,
854: formal-build, marker, or axiom-audit coordinates.  Hence the factorisation and
855: the ``if and only if'' upgrade criterion are exact.
856: \end{proof}
857: 
858: \begin{theorem}[Source-interface semantic-completeness obstruction]
859: \label{thm:source-interface-semantic-completeness-obstruction}
860: Under the source-interface surface certified by
861: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} and
862: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}, every accepted imported
863: discovery-gate premise factors through the five finite fields listed in
864: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}.  In particular, no theorem
865: in the main publication-safety chain may infer semantic completeness of the
866: imported source theory, correctness of the bounded summaries as exact source
867: statements, Lean/BEDC rebuild success, marker correctness, or axiom purity from
868: that surface.  A package claim with any one of those stronger meanings is
869: accepted by the same finite-record calculus if and only if the support surface is
870: extended by a nonempty machine-readable source-interface coordinate appropriate
871: to the stronger meaning: exact source-text import requires the exact source text
872: and a byte-equality or digest-binding comparison against the pinned snapshot;
873: public-source readings require a public fetch-and-compare record with byte
874: equality; formal-source readings require a Lean/BEDC rebuild record with command,
875: source state, environment, exit code, log path, and assigned pass row; and semantic
876: source-theory readings require a semantic-validation record naming the checker or
877: evaluator contract, produced record, digest binding, and assigned pass row.
878: \end{theorem}
879: 
880: \begin{proof}
881: Let $F(I)$ denote the finite projection consisting of the five fields displayed
882: in Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}.  That theorem proves
883: that $F(I)$ is the strongest positive imported-source premise available to
884: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} from the submitted manual and
885: executable source-interface records.  Therefore any accepted consequence using
886: an imported discovery-gate label is a function of $F(I)$.
887: 
888: Now consider any stronger semantic reading, for instance exact source-statement
889: import or marker correctness.  There are two possible review states with the
890: same $F(I)$: in both states the path, commit, labels, line ranges, bounded
891: summaries, JSON equality, bundle-manifest agreement, digest row, line count, and
892: label containment checks agree; in one state an additional semantic extractor or
893: proof checker succeeds, while in the other no such command has been submitted or
894: the unsubmitted semantic fact is false.  The finite source-interface verifier
895: cannot distinguish these states because the distinguishing coordinate is not in
896: $F(I)$.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} and
897: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-source-interface-non-escalation}, the stronger
898: semantic conclusion is therefore not a consequence of the current interface.
899: 
900: Conversely, if the support surface is enlarged by the relevant finite coordinate
901: listed in the statement and by its assigned pass row, the enlarged coordinate is
902: no longer invisible to the certificate calculus: it becomes an ordinary finite
903: extension of the tuple read by the verifier.  Exact source text is then read only
904: through the submitted text and byte-comparison row; public reachability only
905: through the public fetch-and-compare row; rebuild success only through the
906: formal-build command row; and semantic validity only through the submitted
907: semantic-validation contract and produced record.  The acceptance of the
908: strengthened source-interface reading is therefore exactly the extension case of
909: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, applied to the enlarged
910: support surface.  Because at least one source-text, byte-equality,
911: formal-rebuild, semantic-validation, digest-binding, or pass-row field is added,
912: the extension is nonempty.  This proves both the obstruction and the stated
913: if-and-only-if upgrade criterion.
914: \end{proof}
915: 
916: \begin{proposition}[Source-interface evidence-level separation]
917: \label{prop:source-interface-evidence-level-separation}
918: Let $r$ be any accepted manuscript claim whose discovery component cites one of
919: the imported labels in Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}.  If $r$ uses only
920: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}, then the imported-label
921: premise of $r$ is accepted only at the manual path-verified bounded-paraphrase
922: level.  If $r$ additionally cites
923: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}, then the same
924: premise is upgraded only by the finite record comparisons listed in that
925: proposition: table--JSON equality, manifest agreement, snapshot digest and line
926: count agreement, and label containment in the declared ranges.  In neither case
927: does the premise become a fresh Lean/BEDC rebuild, a proof of the semantic truth
928: of the imported source theorem, or an unbounded restatement of the source text.
929: Consequently the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, can use the imported
930: discovery-gate spine only through these two explicitly separated evidence
931: levels.
932: \end{proposition}
933: 
934: \begin{proof}
935: The operational evidence level of a cited source-interface premise is determined
936: by the record fields actually available for that premise.  Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}
937: supplies a pinned path, commit, extraction date, label, line range, and bounded
938: summary for each of the ten imported labels, and its proof states that no later
939: use may exceed that bounded summary.  Therefore a claim using only the lemma has
940: exactly the manual path-verified bounded-paraphrase evidence listed there.
941: 
942: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} adds a different
943: finite record: the verifier parses the manuscript table, the JSON mirror, the
944: manifest's pinned-source-snapshot record, and the bundled source snapshot, and it
945: checks the six finite conditions enumerated in its proof.  Those checks upgrade
946: the source-interface premise only with respect to record-file consistency and
947: range containment.  The same proposition expressly excludes semantic truth of
948: the imported theorems and preserves the bounded-paraphrase restriction.  No row
949: in Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}, no field of
950: \path{source_interface_record.json}, and no command-boundary row records a fresh
951: Lean/BEDC rebuild or a semantic source-theorem verifier.  Hence an imported-label
952: premise has exactly the two evidence levels stated in the proposition and no
953: stronger one.
954: 
955: The finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, requires a discovery-gate source
956: interface with recorded evidence surface and boundary.  Applying the preceding
957: paragraphs to each of its imported discovery-gate citations gives precisely the
958: manual path-verified interface, optionally strengthened by the finite executable
959: record comparison, and forbids promotion of that interface into an unrecorded
960: semantic or rebuild claim.\end{proof}
961: 
962: \section{Operational interface}
963: 
964: \paragraph{Reader map.}
965: The supplement separates one central theorem chain from support-boundary audit
966: records.  The core finite-record safety argument is:
967: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} fixes the certificate tuple,
968: claim kinds, gate dispatch, verifier predicate, evidence levels, and
969: record-gate-soundness hypothesis;
970: Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface} isolates that
971: hypothesis as an external finite input/output interface, and
972: Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness} proves
973: that it has no hidden semantic or venue-acceptance content;
974: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} restates the finite
975: acceptance predicates as the table relation \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\);
976: Theorems~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract},
977: \ref{thm:finite-verifier-normal-form},
978: \ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, and
979: \ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} prove verifier exactness and
980: non-escalation from those predicates;